[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0, leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not a failed link but a routing policy gap: eBGP ECMP is not enabled on the leaf routers. On leaf_router_0_0, “show ip bgp 10.0.1.0/24” shows two valid external paths (via 172.16.0.4 and 172.16.0.8), but the FIB has only a single next-hop installed: “B>* 10.0.1.0/24 … via 172.16.0.4, eth0”. Its BGP config lacks any “maximum-paths” or “bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax.” The same is true in the reverse direction on leaf_router_0_1 for 10.0.0.0/24 (only via 172.16.0.6). This means traffic is always polarized to one spine based on BGP bestpath tiebreaks (router-id), not load-balanced.

Mechanism: With only one next-hop in the kernel, any transient issue on that chosen spine path causes end-to-end loss until BGP reconverges to the other spine, matching the report of “all traffic on a single dead path.” Current pings succeed because the selected spine path is up now, but ECMP is still absent.

Localization: FRR BGP on the leafs (leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1), IPv4 unicast address-family.

Root cause: ECMP was never enabled for eBGP on the leaf routers, so only a single best path is programmed.

Corrective fix: On both leaf routers, under router bgp -> address-family ipv4 unicast, configure:
- maximum-paths 2
- bgp bestpath as-path multipath-relax
This will install both spine next-hops. No device restart is required; FRR applies these live. If desired, you may reload FRR on both leafs after committing the change, but it is not necessary.
=== END ===